<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Taylor Series and its convergence</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The main objectives of this section are to answer the following questions:
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Under what conditions, the function <m> f(x) </m> is equal to the Taylor series generated 
            by <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>? That is, when can we say 
            <m>f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!} (x-a)^n</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If the Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m>, <m>p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k</m> , is used as an 
            approximation to <m>f(x)</m>, what is the error in this approximation? 
            That is, what is the value of <m>R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

  </objectives>

   <subsection xml:id="subsection-1">
    <title> Taylor's Formula and The Remainder Estimation Theorem </title>
    <theorem xml:id="Taylor-Formula">
      <title>Taylor's Formula</title>
      
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on an open interval 
          <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each <m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a number 
          <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
          <me>
            f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x),
          </me>
          where 
          <me>
            p_n(x)= \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k,
          </me>
          and
        <me>
          R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}.
        </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof of the remainder theorem is based on the mean value theorem and Taylor's theorem and we have included 
        the proof in <xref ref="subsection-5"/>. The proof is postponed to the end of this section because 
        it requires some technical details that are not necessary for understanding the main ideas of this section. 
        <alert> The students are not expected to know the proof, however, they should understand the statement and its 
          implications. Interested students are encouraged to read the proof.</alert> 
      </p>
    </proof>
    <remark>
  <title>Finding an upper bound for the error term without knowing the value of <m>c</m></title>
  <p>
  Usually the value of <m>c</m> is not explicitly known. However, we may manage to find <alert>an upper bound</alert> 
   for the error term <m>R_n(x)</m> without knowing the exact value of <m>c</m>. This is achieved by finding an upper bound <m> M</m>
   for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m>, where <m>c</m> is between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, and then using this upper bound to estimate the error term.
  </p>
</remark>

 <theorem xml:id="thm-remainder-theorem">
      <title>The Remainder Estimation Theorem</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on an open interval 
          <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each <m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a number 
          <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
          <me>
            |R_n(x)| \leq \frac{M}{(n+1)!} |x-a|^{n+1},
          </me>
          where <m>M</m> is an upper bound for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the interval between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof of the remainder estimation theorem is based on the Taylor's formula and the fact that 
        <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)| \leq M</m> for some upper bound <m>M</m>. Therefore, we have
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_n(x)| = \left| \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1} \right| \leq \frac{M}{(n+1)!} |x-a|^{n+1}.
      </me> 
    </proof>

<example>
      <title>Finding an upper bound for the error term</title>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>. We want to approximate <m>e^{0.5}</m> 
        using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at <m>x=0</m>. The Taylor polynomial is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_2(x) = 1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2}
      </me>
      <p>
        The error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_2(0.5) = \frac{f^{(3)}(c)}{3!} (0.5)^3= \frac{e^c}{6} (0.5)^3
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>c</m> is between <m>0</m> and <m>0.5</m>, we have <m>0 \leq c \leq 0.5</m>. 
        Therefore, we can bound the remainder term as follows:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_2(0.5) \leq \frac{e^{0.5}}{6} (0.5)^3 \approx 0.03435
      </me>
      <p>
        This shows that the error in approximating <m>e^{0.5}</m> using the Taylor polynomial 
        of degree 2 is less than <m>0.0026</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
      In fact, if we compute the actual value of <m>e^{0.5}</m> and compare it with the approximation
      given by <m>p_2(0.5)</m>, we find that the actual error is <m>e^{0.5} - p_2(0.5) \approx 0.02372</m>, which 
      is indeed less than the upper bound we calculated.
      </p>
       <figure xml:id="fig-geogebra-Taylor1">
    <caption>
      The red curve is the graph of <m>f(x) = e^x</m> and 
      the blue curve is the graph of the Taylor polynomial <m>p_2(x)</m>. 
      The exact value of <m>e^{0.5}</m> is represented by the red point and 
      the approximation given by <m>p_2(0.5)</m> is represented by the blue point. 
      The vertical distance between the two points represents the error in the approximation. 
      Note that the error is less than the upper bound we calculated, i.e., <m>0.03435</m>.
    </caption>
    <interactive geogebra="xzdc5jpq" width="95%" shift-drag-zoom="no" aspect="16:11"/>
  </figure>
      </example>
      


  </subsection>
 
    <subsection xml:id="subsection-2">
    <title>An example of a Taylor series that converges</title>
    <p>
      Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>. We know that <m>f^{(n)}(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>n\geq 0</m>. 
      Therefore, the Taylor series generated by <m>f</m> at <m>x=0</m> is given by:
    </p> 
    <me>
      \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!} x^n = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n!} x^n
    </me>
    <p>
      We will show that this series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x\in \R</m>. To do this, we will use the remainder theorem. We have:
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1} = \frac{e^c}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      First, we consider the case when <m>x>0</m>. Since <m>c</m> is between <m>0</m> and <m>x</m>, 
      we have <m>0 \leq c \leq x</m>. Therefore, we can bound the remainder term as follows:    
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) \leq \frac{e^x}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      Now we can compute the limit of the remainder term as <m>n\to \infty</m>:
    </p>
    <me>
      \lim_{n\to \infty} |R_n(x)| \leq \lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{e^x}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1} = 0
    </me>
    <p>
      This shows that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x>0</m>. 
      The second case is when <m>x &lt; 0</m>. In this case, since <m>c</m> is between <m>x</m> and <m>0</m>,
      we have <m>x \leq c \leq 0</m>. Hence, we can bound the remainder term as follows:
    </p>
    <me>
      |R_n(x)| \leq \frac{1}{(n+1)!} |x|^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      Now we can compute the limit of the remainder term as <m>n\to \infty</m>:
    </p>
    <me>
      \lim_{n\to \infty} |R_n(x)| \leq \lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{1}{(n+1)!} |x|^{n+1} = 0
    </me>
    <p>
      This shows that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x &lt; 0</m>. Therefore, 
      we conclude that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x\in \R</m>.
    </p>    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsection-3">
    <title> The error in using a Taylor polynomial</title>
    <p>
      In this subsection, we will discuss how to approximate the error in using a Taylor polynomial to approximate a function. 
      We will use the remainder theorem to bound the error term <m>R_n(x)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose we want to approximate <m>f(x)</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m> at <m>x=a</m>. 
      The error in this approximation is given by <m>R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>. By the remainder theorem, we have:
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      To bound the error term, we need to find an upper bound for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the interval between 
      <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.
    </p> 
    <example>
      <title>Approximating <m>e^x</m> using a Taylor polynomial</title>
      <p>
        Approximate <m>e^{0.5}</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at <m>x=0</m>. 
        Find an upper bound for the error in this approximation.
      </p>
      </example>
      <solution>
      <p>
        We will approximate <m>f(x) = e^x</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at <m>x=0</m>:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_2(x) = 1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2}
      </me>
      <p> 
        We want to approximate <m>e^{0.5}</m>. The error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_2(0.5) = \frac{f^{(3)}(c)}{3!} (0.5)^3
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(3)}(x) = e^x</m>, we have:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_2(0.5)| \leq \frac{e^{0.5}}{6} (0.5)^3
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, the error in approximating <m>e^{0.5}</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 is bounded by:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_2(0.5)| \leq \frac{e^{0.5}}{6} (0.125) \approx 0.034
      </me>
    </solution>

    <example> 
      <title>Approximating <m>\sin(x)</m> using a Taylor polynomial</title>
      <p>
        Assume that we use the Taylor polynomial of degree 3 at <m>x=0</m> to approximate <m>\sin(x)</m>. For approximately what values of <m>x</m> 
        can you replace <m>\sin(x)</m> by such a Taylor polynomial with an error of magnitude no greater than <m> 4 \times 10^{-3}</m>?
      </p>
    </example>  
    <solution>
      <p>
        The Taylor polynomial of degree 3 at <m>x=0</m> for <m>\sin(x)</m> is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_3(x) = x - \frac{x^3}{6}
      </me>
      <p>
       Since  p_3(x) = p_4(x), the error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_4(x) = \frac{f^{(5)}(c)}{5!} x^5
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(5)}(x) = \cos(x)</m>, we have:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_4(x)| \leq \frac{|x|^5}{120}
      </me>
      <p>
        We want to find the values of <m>x</m> such that:
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{|x|^5}{120} \leq 4 \times 10^{-3}
      </me>
      <p>
        This gives us:
      </p>
      <me>
        |x|^5 \leq 0.48
      </me>
      <p>
        Taking the fifth root, we get:
      </p>
      <me>
        |x| \leq 0.86347
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, we can replace <m>\sin(x)</m> by the Taylor polynomial of degree 3 
        at <m>x=0</m> with an error of magnitude no greater than 
        <m> 4 \times 10^{-3}</m> for <m>|x| \leq 0.86347</m>.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-geogebra-Taylor2A">
    <caption>
     The red curve is the graph of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> 
     and the blue curve is the graph of the Taylor polynomial <m>p_3(x)= x - \frac{x^3}{6}</m>. 
     Use the slider to change the value of <m>x</m> and see how the error changes for different 
     values of <m>x</m>. Note that for the slider values have been limited to fit the page. 
     </caption>
    <interactive geogebra="ptgj7hpk" material-width="790" material-height="500" 
    shift-drag-zoom="no" aspect="16:10"/>
  </figure>
       <figure xml:id="fig-geogebra-Taylor2B">
    <caption>
     Use the slider to change the value of <m>x_0</m> and see how the error changes. 
     Note that for <m>|x_0| \leq 0.86347</m>, the error is less than <m> 4 \times 10^{-3}</m>.
    </caption>
    <interactive geogebra="z854nrgd" material-width="736" material-height="423" shift-drag-zoom="no" aspect="16:9"/>
  </figure>

    </solution>
  
  <example>
    <title> Finding the value of <m>n</m> such that the error is less than a given tolerance</title>
    <p>
      Find the smallest value of <m>n</m> for which the polynomial approximation for <m>\sin(x)</m> 
      is accurate to <m>±0.005</m> for values of <m>x</m> in the interval <m>[-\pi/2, \pi/2]</m>.
      </p>
      </example>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m> at <m>x=0</m> for <m>\sin(x)</m> is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{(-1)^k}{(2k+1)!} x^{2k+1}
      </me>
      <p>
        The error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(n+1)}(x)</m> is either <m>\cos(x)</m> or <m>\sin(x)</m>, we have:  
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_n(x)| \leq \frac{|x|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}
      </me>
      <p>
        We want to find the smallest value of <m>n</m> such that:
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{|x|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!} \leq 0.005
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>|x| \leq \pi/2</m> for <m>x \in [-\pi/2, \pi/2]</m>, and approximating 
        <m>\pi/2 \approx 2</m>, we have:
      </p>
        <me>
        \frac{|x|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!} \lt \frac{2^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}
        </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, we want to find the smallest value of <m>n</m> such that:  
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{2^{n+1}}{(n+1)!} \lt 0.005
      </me>
      <p>
        We can check the values of <m>n</m> starting from <m>n=0</m> until 
       we find the smallest value of <m>n</m> that satisfies the inequality. 
       After checking, we find that the smallest value of <m>n</m> that satisfies 
       the inequality is <m>n=8</m>. 
      </p>
      <interactive desmos="7b85e78f17" width="70%" aspect="1:2" />
      </solution> 
      
</subsection>

  
  <subsection>
    <title> Applications of Taylor's Formula</title>
    <p>
      In this subsection, we will discuss some applications of Taylor's formula. 
      We will see how Taylor's formula can be used to study the period of a pendulum and how 
      newtonian mechanics is an approximation of relativistic mechanics when the velocity of 
      an object is much smaller than the speed of light.
    </p>
    <subsubsection>
      <title> The period of a pendulum</title>
      <p>
        The period of a pendulum is given by the formula:
      </p>
      <me>
        T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} \left( 1 + \frac{1}{16} \theta_0^2 + \frac{11}{3072} \theta_0^4 + ... \right)
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>L</m> is the length of the pendulum, <m>g</m> is the acceleration due to gravity, and <m>\theta_0</m> is the maximum angular displacement of the pendulum. 
        The series in parentheses is a Taylor series expansion of the function <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - k^2 \sin^2(\theta/2)}}</m> evaluated at <m>\theta = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection>
      <title> Newtonian mechanics as an approximation of relativistic mechanics</title>
      <p>
        In relativistic mechanics, the kinetic energy of an object is given by the formula:
      </p>
      <me>
        K = mc^2 \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 -(v/c)^2}} - 1 \right)
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>m</m> is the mass of the object, <m>c</m> is the speed of light, and <m>v</m> is the velocity of the object. When <m>v</m> is much smaller than <m>c</m>, we can use Taylor's formula to approximate the kinetic energy as:
      </p>
      <me>
        K \approx \frac{1}{2} mv^2
      </me>
      <p>
        This shows that Newtonian mechanics is an approximation of relativistic mechanics when the velocity of an object is much smaller than the speed of light.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
    
    

  <subsection xml:id="subsection-5">
    <title> Proof of the remainder theorem</title>
    <p>
      In this subsection, we will provide a proof of the remainder theorem.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on an open interval
       <m>I</m> containing <m>a </m>. We want to show that for each <m>x\in I</m> and for each 
       positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
    </p>
    <me>
      f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x),
    </me>
    <p>
      where
    </p>
    <me>
      p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k,
    </me>
    <p>
      and
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}.
    </me>
    <p>
      To prove this, we will first prove the following lemma:
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lemma-1">
      <statement>   
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is <m>(n+1)</m>-times differentiable. 
          Also, suppose that <m>f(a) = f'(a) = f''(a) = ... = f^{(n)}(a) = 0</m> and <m>f(x) = 0</m>, where <m>x\neq a</m>.
          Then there exists a number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that 
          <m>f^{(n+1)}(c) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement> 
    </lemma>
    <proof> 
      <p>
        The proof of this lemma is based on the mean value theorem. 
        Since <m>f(a) = f'(a) = f''(a) = ... = f^{(n)}(a) = 0</m>, we can apply the mean value theorem 
        repeatedly to show that there exists a number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that 
        <m>f^{(n+1)}(c) = 0</m>. Below we will do so step by step. 
      </p>
      <p>
        First, since <m>f(a) = f(x) = 0</m>, by the mean value theorem, there exists a number <m>c_1</m> between 
        <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that 
      </p>
      <me>
        f'(c_1) = \frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} = 0
      </me>
      <p>
       Second, since <m>f'(a) = f'(c_1) = 0</m>, by the mean value theorem, there exists a number <m>c_2</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>c_1</m> such that 
      </p>
      <me>
        f''(c_2) = \frac{f'(c_1) - f'(a)}{c_1 - a} = 0
      </me>
      <p>
        Then by continuing this process, we can show that there exists a number 
        <m>c_n</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>c_{n-1}</m> such that 
      </p>
      <me>
        f^{(n)}(c_n) = 0
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(n)}(a) = f^{(n)}(c_n) = 0</m>, by the mean value theorem, there exists a number <m>c_{n+1}</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>c_n</m> such that 
      </p>
      <me>
        f^{(n+1)}(c_{n+1}) = 0
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, we have shown that there exists a number <m>c = c_{n+1}</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that 
        <m>f^{(n+1)}(c) = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
    <remark xml:id="rmk-zero-error-function">  
      <title>The error function and its derivatives are zero at the point of expansion</title>
    <p>
      Note that for the error function  <m> R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x) </m>, we have 
      <m>R_n(a) = R_n'(a) = R_n''(a) = ... = R_n^{(n)}(a) = 0</m>.
    </p>
    </remark>
    <p>
      Now, assume that <m>x\neq a</m>. We can construct the function 
      <m>g(t) = R_n(t) - \frac{R_n(x)}{(x-a)^{n+1}} (t-a)^{n+1}</m> 
      such that <m>g(a) = g(x) = 0</m> and <m>g^{(k)}(a) = 0</m> for <m>k = 1, 2, ..., n</m>. 
      Therefore, by the lemma and considering <xref ref="rmk-zero-error-function"/>, there exists a number <m>c</m> between 
      <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that <m>g^{(n+1)}(c)=0</m>. After computing  <m>g^{(n+1)}(c)</m>, we have
      <m>R_n^{(n+1)}(c) - \frac{R_n(x)}{(x-a)^{n+1}} (n+1)! = 0</m>. Since <m>R_n^{(n+1)}(c) = f^{(n+1)}(c)</m>, we have
      <m>R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}</m>. This completes the proof of the remainder theorem.
    </p>
    </subsection>
    

